WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.330
- My next guest is an
award-winning journalist

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:03.550
we all know and love.

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:06.450
In her new BET show Disrupt &amp; Dismantle,

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:08.970
she takes a deeper look at systemic racism

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:12.050
and the steps we can take
to achieve lasting change.

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:13.420
Check it out.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.180
- Attacking the issues head on.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.910
- Children are out here, and
fighting for their lives.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:18.810
- [Soledad O'Brien] Making it right.

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.221
Is it as simple as money?

00:00:20.221 --> 00:00:21.054
- Yes.

00:00:21.054 --> 00:00:21.887
We need more funding.

00:00:21.887 --> 00:00:22.720
- [Soledad O'Brien] Real action.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.690
- She was absolutely denied
her basic human rights.

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:29.360
- [Soledad O'Brien] Standing
by is no longer an option.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:30.360
- This shouldn't be happening.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:31.950
And it's just wrong.

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:33.420
- [Voiceover] Disrupt and Dismantle,

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.383
hosted by Soledad O'Brien.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.985
- Wow. Please welcome
Soledad O'Brien everybody.

00:00:38.985 --> 00:00:42.083
(upbeat talk show music)

00:00:42.083 --> 00:00:45.083
(audience cheering)

00:00:51.894 --> 00:00:53.440
- I matched it to the blue on your dress.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:54.570
- [Kelly Clarkson] I literally,

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:56.270
I literally just painted a,

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:57.830
my whole, my living room that color

00:00:57.830 --> 00:00:59.542
'cause I think it's such a
great, peaceful great color.

00:00:59.542 --> 00:01:00.430
- Right.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:01.263
- Yeah.

00:01:01.263 --> 00:01:02.096
- And it brightens everything up.

00:01:02.096 --> 00:01:02.929
- You look beautiful.

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:03.762
- Thank you. Likewise.

00:01:03.762 --> 00:01:05.030
And likewise. And likewise.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:06.070
- Thank you for coming on the show.

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:08.470
So what made you wanna
do a show like this?

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:09.500
You wanna see change?

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:10.460
- [Soledad O'Brien] Yeah, and I think

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.220
we felt the time was right.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:16.020
I think in the, the wake of
the killing of George Floyd,

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.100
I noticed, and I think a
lot of other people did too,

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:21.040
that the conversation
around race, and racism

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:22.060
was, was changing.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:24.780
That people across the
board, not just black people,

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.900
but all people were like,
we need to figure this out.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.880
- No, and it's actually a
really important time I think

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.140
to do it right now because
I think a lot of people

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:34.040
that didn't know things are happening.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:35.850
Like that, you can't change them,

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:37.940
unless you know it exists, you know?

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:40.310
But you say it's, it's
important to highlight

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:42.490
how systems are often
the root of injustice,

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:43.470
so can you explain that?

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:44.920
- Yeah, well systems are complicated.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.017
Right, in a way, it's
almost easier to say,

00:01:47.017 --> 00:01:50.320
"Well that person did this,
and that person did this."

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.620
But systems are big and overwhelming

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:56.780
and how do you think about
the system that keeps people

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:57.990
in an unequal plane?

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:00.160
For example, my mom and
dad came to this country.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:02.450
My mom was Afro-Cuban, black and Cuban.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:04.160
My dad was white and Australian.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:05.670
When they moved into their neighborhood,

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:07.050
no one would sell them a house

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:09.470
because they would not allow black people

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:11.390
to move into their neighborhood.

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:12.770
That, that's a system, right.

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:14.010
That's not even individuals.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:16.690
That's a system that changed over time.

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:18.320
And so we wanted to both look at,

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:20.630
at history, what put
these systems in place,

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:22.950
and then also look at,
okay well if it's a system

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:25.990
and it's big and complicated,
I think there's a real

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:28.236
tendency to be like, "Well that's just.

00:02:28.236 --> 00:02:29.069
That's overwhelming."

00:02:29.069 --> 00:02:29.902
- [Kelly Clarkson] Overwhelming, yeah.

00:02:29.902 --> 00:02:31.380
- I can't manage that by myself.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.980
And we wanted to show
people how you could disrupt

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:35.950
and dismantle systems.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:37.850
What specific things you could do.

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:39.210
- [Kelly Clarkson] I
actually love that about this

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:42.190
because you're not only
providing what the problems are,

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:44.150
you're providing solutions right?

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:46.020
- Yeah, and it's really
easy to admire problems

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.110
like yeah, well, that is bad.

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:49.050
That is depressing.

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:50.840
- [Kelly Clarkson] But like
what the hell do you do?

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:51.830
- [Soledad O'Brien] Exactly. Exactly.

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:55.180
And so we wanted to provide
a real, you know, map.

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:57.630
There are ways, and
sometimes those ways are,

00:02:57.630 --> 00:02:59.050
get involved in politics,

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:00.300
figure out zoning.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.280
Understand who you're
elected officials are.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:03.220
Can you name them?

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:04.850
Do you have their numbers?

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:06.760
What are your rights
in terms of what you're

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:08.320
trying to change in your community.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:09.730
- [Kelly Clarkson] Yeah. Agreed, agreed.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:12.210
Well, one of the episodes
focuses on infant mortality.

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:13.430
So can you tell us more about that?

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:14.263
- [Soledad O'Brien] Yeah.

00:03:14.263 --> 00:03:15.980
You know, if you look at the disparity

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:18.530
between black women and
white women when it comes

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:21.530
to maternal mortality,
and infant mortality.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:22.410
- [Kelly Clarkson] I have
actually heard of this.

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:24.440
- The numbers are terrible.

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:27.300
Black women are three to
four times more likely,

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:31.110
three to four times more
likely to die in childbirth.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:32.490
It mean, it's, it's crazy.

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:34.380
So we wanted to understand that.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:37.100
And what you really see
pretty clearly pretty quickly

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:40.180
is that there are these sort of racial

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:44.540
and systemic racism that
sort of influences outcomes

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:46.310
for women and also their infants as well,

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:48.830
the infant mortality
numbers are terrible too.

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:50.800
And so we wanted to
explore what was happening

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:51.840
around infant mortality.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.760
The stories are terrible,
and they go from women

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:56.960
not getting good access to health care,

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:59.240
women living in healthcare deserts,

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:00.630
where they're just not anywhere close

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:02.610
to good places to get healthcare.

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:05.540
And also, a lot of black
women reported doctors

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:08.330
just not really listening to them.

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:10.690
There're lots of studies
that say doctors and others

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:14.230
believe black women have a
higher tolerance for pain.

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:15.470
So we're really interested

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:17.260
in exploring, well what
exactly is involved?

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:19.840
Sometimes it's just access
to, to better systems

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:21.700
around funding for healthcare,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:23.470
So we looked at this and thought,

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:26.100
okay, here's the problem,
it's fully documented.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.910
We know a lot of the
reasons. How do you solve it?

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:31.890
There was nothing unusual
about Kim's pregnancy,

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:33.850
and the delivery was normal.

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:36.570
Yet, three days after giving birth,

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:38.993
Kim's health took a drastic turn.

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:42.090
- [Family Member] Like
she couldn't see well,

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:44.080
and she kept having a lot of headaches.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:47.070
We had talked to this doctor
about sending her to Memphis

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:48.810
several times trying to figure out

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:50.160
what was going on with her.

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:53.563
But he decided not to send her.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.423
So she continued to deteriorate.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:00.930
- Wow, welcome back.

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:03.120
That was a clip from Disrupt &amp; Dismantle.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:05.483
And I'm here with the host
of the show Soledad O'Brien.

00:05:05.483 --> 00:05:06.995
Give it up for her everybody.

00:05:06.995 --> 00:05:09.180
(audience applauding)

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:11.460
So in that episode, Soledad examined

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:13.950
the infant mortality rates in Mississippi.

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:15.980
We have two of the women from that episode

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:16.890
joining us right now.

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:20.291
Everybody please welcome
sisters Notoya and Nikitra.

00:05:20.291 --> 00:05:22.170
(audience applauding)

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:23.003
Hi ladies.

00:05:23.864 --> 00:05:24.770
- Hi.

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:25.603
- Hey.

00:05:25.603 --> 00:05:26.660
- Well first of all, I
have to start with this,

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:28.490
you both look beautiful with
the colors you're wearing

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:31.220
and your smiles are gorgeous.

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:32.560
- Oh well, thank you.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:33.800
- Yeah. Absolutely.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:35.660
I was like, "Wow, y'all
look like little lights

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:37.140
on the screen."

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:38.810
Well Notoya, I'll, I'll start with you.

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:40.810
You started researching infant mortality

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:42.230
after losing your baby, right?

00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:44.270
Can you tell us what happened?

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:45.103
- [Notoya] Yeah sure.

00:05:45.103 --> 00:05:49.100
So in February of 2016,

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:52.330
I was about 25 weeks pregnant at the time,

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:56.210
and I started experiencing
some like upper-quadrant pain.

00:05:56.210 --> 00:05:59.490
So fast forward, you know, we
went to the emergency room.

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:01.900
Of course I was quickly seen,

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:06.310
but in a matter of literally minutes,

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:08.020
we were, you know, we were, we realized

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:10.990
that things were taking
a turn for the worst.

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:13.890
I was told that I would be transferred

00:06:13.890 --> 00:06:15.773
to Jackson Mississippi,

00:06:16.670 --> 00:06:19.300
which is two hours away from Starkville.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:21.550
And I would probably deliver there

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:23.820
or stay there until delivery.

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:26.080
And so, I later found out that

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:28.140
the University of
Mississippi Medical Center

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:31.010
is the only level four NICU in this state.

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:34.030
And two days later, I delivered Jordan

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:38.020
via emergency C-Section
at 25 weeks, one day,

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:41.360
weighing a whopping one
pound and one ounce.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:42.193
- [Kelly Clarkson] Wow.

00:06:42.193 --> 00:06:45.330
- [Notoya] And so, after Jordan
passed at eight months old,

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:47.060
my husband and I started
thinking about, you know,

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:49.250
we knew that we couldn't
prevent this from happening

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:52.370
to another child, but if
we could ease the burden

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:54.650
of a family going through it,

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:56.540
then that's what, you know, we were like,

00:06:56.540 --> 00:06:59.060
maybe we were chosen for
this path for a reason

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:00.960
and so what will we do going forward?

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:03.150
And so in combination with the

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:05.070
University of Mississippi Medical Center

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:08.930
in Jackson Mississippi, we
formed a family advisory board.

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:09.970
And so it's made up

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:13.230
of current NICU parents,
former NICU parents.

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:16.960
Nurses, hospital
administration, social workers.

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:19.390
And we basically serve as a support

00:07:19.390 --> 00:07:22.470
for, you know, parents going
through the NICU journey.

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:23.303
- [Kelly Clarkson] That's amazing.

00:07:23.303 --> 00:07:25.100
I was literally gonna
say, "Power in numbers."

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:26.610
Like making that, having that network

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:28.970
of people that support from, from people,

00:07:28.970 --> 00:07:30.540
especially from the professionals
that you have in that,

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:32.410
that network environment too, telling you,

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:35.000
you know their expertise,
this might be happening,

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:35.940
this might be, you know what I'm saying?

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:37.973
Like having that group to kind of go to.

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:41.620
Nikitra you, you've looked at
the infant mortality crisis

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:43.890
from another perspective right?

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:44.723
- [Nikitra] Yeah.

00:07:44.723 --> 00:07:48.700
So, I, I've been in public
health for about 13 years now,

00:07:48.700 --> 00:07:50.480
and most of my career has been

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:52.590
in sexual and reproductive health.

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:54.790
And so, when you think
about reproductive health,

00:07:54.790 --> 00:07:57.040
that of course includes
reproductive rights,

00:07:57.040 --> 00:07:59.370
and pregnancy labor delivery.

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:02.080
I kind of took my family
lens off and started trying

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:04.750
to look at this from a
public health perspective

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:06.860
because this wasn't just
a strange turn of events

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:10.600
for our family, it's
happening across the country.

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:12.870
And so, I was trying to figure out how,

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:14.020
how do we fix this?

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:15.500
What do we do?

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:18.770
And so I started looking at the system.

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:20.540
And that's where the issue lies.

00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:25.050
We can tell a woman to say, "I'm hurting.

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:27.570
I need this. I feel this way."

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:29.940
But if a doctor's not listening to her,

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:31.100
then that's a problem.

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:33.060
If we have our moms healthy,

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:34.930
then we can get our babies healthy.

00:08:34.930 --> 00:08:36.780
But, in order to do that,

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:39.970
we really have to address systemic racism,

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:43.010
and all of those barriers
that keep a woman

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:45.300
from being able to navigate that system

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:47.670
of healthcare in a way that gets her out

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:49.030
on the other side and gives her

00:08:49.030 --> 00:08:51.470
this great experience of childbirth.

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:53.690
We, we always see childbirth in this,

00:08:53.690 --> 00:08:55.450
you know, this great experience.

00:08:55.450 --> 00:08:58.090
But, it's so complex for black mothers

00:08:58.090 --> 00:09:00.210
that we don't get that opportunity.

00:09:00.210 --> 00:09:02.840
And so that's the lens
that I look at it from.

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:04.830
- That's what I'm, that's
why it's unfortunate

00:09:04.830 --> 00:09:06.570
that doctors really don't listen

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:08.810
because it is so different.

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:10.180
Even with the same woman,

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:11.900
my two pregnancies were very different.

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:14.470
You have no idea, like
what's, what's happening.

00:09:14.470 --> 00:09:16.440
And it could be a, a plethora of,

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:18.260
of things, you know, happening.

00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:20.650
So I, I'm, I'm so sorry and I,

00:09:20.650 --> 00:09:21.483
I wanna say this so.

00:09:21.483 --> 00:09:23.690
Soledad, you, you see hope that,

00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:26.180
the infant mortality rate
can be reduced, right?

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:28.320
- Yeah, look at these two sisters

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:30.410
who could be doing a 100 other things

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:32.420
and instead have decided
to dedicate their lives

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:33.890
to making change, because they know

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:35.080
it's not just their story,

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:36.410
it's the story of a lot of black women.

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:38.660
A lot of women, and a lot of black women.

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:41.090
And I think there's always
hope when you have people

00:09:41.090 --> 00:09:42.340
first,

00:09:42.340 --> 00:09:44.190
deciding to have a difficult conversation.

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:45.170
What is the data?

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:46.070
What are the facts?

00:09:46.070 --> 00:09:46.903
What do we know?

00:09:46.903 --> 00:09:48.590
In Mississippi, they track this now,

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:51.270
because people deserve
to understand the data.

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:53.210
And really it's what our
whole entire show is about.

00:09:53.210 --> 00:09:54.930
We deserve to understand the data,

00:09:54.930 --> 00:09:55.990
and after you do that,

00:09:55.990 --> 00:09:59.500
you can say, "Okay, now we
know, what do we do to change?"

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:01.430
- [Kelly Clarkson] Yeah. And
how is information ever bad?

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:03.110
And that's my thing, is
like, the more information

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:05.860
we, the more conversations,
